\section{Method: Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning}
\label{sec:method}

We propose \textbf{Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning (\rasp{})}, a three-stage framework that optimizes structural pruning decisions for post-RLVR recoverability rather than immediate accuracy preservation.
The core insight is that structures with high functional overlap---those whose computations are redundant with other retained structures---are more recoverable under RLVR, because reinforcement learning can redistribute their function through global exploration~\citep{rlsqueezes2026}.

\subsection{Preliminaries and Notation}
\label{sec:prelim}

\paragraph{Structured pruning.}
Given a pretrained transformer $\mathcal{M}$ with $L$ layers, structured pruning removes entire computational units---attention heads or MLP channels---to reduce model size while maintaining hardware-friendly dense computation.
We denote the set of prunable structures as $\mathcal{H} = \{h_1, h_2, \ldots, h_N\}$.

\paragraph{Grouped Query Attention (GQA).}
In GQA architectures~\citep{ainslie2023gqa}, $n_Q$ query heads share $n_{KV}$ key-value heads, with each KV head serving a group of $G = n_Q / n_{KV}$ query heads.
For DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B ($n_Q{=}28$, $n_{KV}{=}4$), $G = 7$, yielding 4 GQA groups per layer.
We treat each GQA group (7 Q heads + 1 KV head) as a single prunable unit, since removing a KV head necessitates removing all associated Q heads.

\paragraph{RLVR and GRPO.}
Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) recovers pruned model capabilities using outcome-based rewards from tasks with verifiable answers.
We use Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO;~\citealp{shao2024grpo}) as the recovery algorithm, following~\citet{selfhealing2025}.

\subsection{Stage 1: Reasoning-Trace Structural Analysis}
\label{sec:stage1}

\rasp{} computes two complementary scores per structure: \emph{importance} $I(h)$ capturing the structure's contribution to reasoning, and \emph{functional overlap} $F(h)$ measuring the degree to which its function is recoverable from other structures.

\paragraph{Calibration data.}
We compute importance and overlap scores on chain-of-thought reasoning data from GSM8K~\citep{cobbe2021gsm8k} training examples, using question--answer pairs as calibration traces.
Given a calibration set $\mathcal{D}_\text{cal} = \{(x_j, y_j)\}_{j=1}^{M}$, we compute activations and gradients on the concatenated sequences $(x_j, y_j)$.
This ensures importance scores reflect the model's behavior on representative reasoning tasks.

\paragraph{Structural importance.}
For each structure $h$, we compute importance $I(h)$ via first-order Taylor expansion on the reasoning trace loss:
\begin{equation}
    I(h) = \left| \sum_{(x,y) \in \mathcal{D}_\text{cal}} \mathbf{a}_h(x, y)^\top \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}(x, y)}{\partial \mathbf{a}_h} \right|,
    \label{eq:importance}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{a}_h$ denotes the activation of structure $h$ and $\mathcal{L}$ is the language modeling loss over the reasoning trace.
For a GQA group $g$ containing Q heads $\{q_1, \ldots, q_G\}$ and KV head $k$, we aggregate: $I(g) = \sum_{i=1}^{G} I(q_i) + I(k)$.

\paragraph{Functional overlap via CKA.}
We measure functional overlap using linear Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA;~\citealp{kornblith2019cka}), which quantifies similarity between two sets of representations invariant to invertible linear transformations.
Given activation matrices $\mathbf{X}, \mathbf{Y} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times d}$ collected over $n$ calibration examples, linear CKA is:
\begin{equation}
    \text{CKA}(\mathbf{X}, \mathbf{Y}) = \frac{\text{HSIC}(\mathbf{X}, \mathbf{Y})}{\sqrt{\text{HSIC}(\mathbf{X}, \mathbf{X}) \cdot \text{HSIC}(\mathbf{Y}, \mathbf{Y})}},
    \label{eq:cka}
\end{equation}
where $\text{HSIC}(\mathbf{X}, \mathbf{Y}) = \frac{1}{(n-1)^2} \operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{K} \mathbf{H} \mathbf{L} \mathbf{H})$, with $\mathbf{K} = \mathbf{X}\mathbf{X}^\top$, $\mathbf{L} = \mathbf{Y}\mathbf{Y}^\top$, and $\mathbf{H} = \mathbf{I}_n - \frac{1}{n}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top$.

For a GQA group $g_i$ in layer $\ell$, we form its representation by concatenating its constituent Q head activations: $\mathbf{X}_{g_i} = [\mathbf{a}_{q_1}; \ldots; \mathbf{a}_{q_G}] \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times Gd_h}$.
The functional overlap of $g_i$ is the mean pairwise CKA with other groups in the same layer:
\begin{equation}
    F(g_i) = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{G}_\ell| - 1} \sum_{\substack{g_j \in \mathcal{G}_\ell \\ j \neq i}} \text{CKA}(\mathbf{X}_{g_i}, \mathbf{X}_{g_j}),
    \label{eq:overlap}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{G}_\ell$ is the set of GQA groups in layer $\ell$.
Unlike prior work that uses inter-layer CKA to identify globally redundant layers for depth pruning~\citep{mpruner2024,lachance2024cka}, \rasp{} uses \emph{intra-layer} CKA between individual GQA groups to predict per-structure recoverability---a fundamentally different granularity and objective.

\paragraph{MLP channel group overlap.}
We apply the same intra-layer CKA overlap to MLP channel groups.
Each MLP intermediate dimension is partitioned into equal-sized groups; for group $m_i$ in layer $\ell$, we compute $F(m_i) = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_\ell| - 1} \sum_{m_j \in \mathcal{M}_\ell, j \neq i} \text{CKA}(\mathbf{X}_{m_i}, \mathbf{X}_{m_j})$, where $\mathcal{M}_\ell$ is the set of MLP groups in layer $\ell$.
Attention GQA groups and MLP channel groups are then scored and ranked jointly in Stage~2.

\subsection{Stage 2: Recovery-Aware Scoring}
\label{sec:stage2}

The recovery-aware score combines importance and functional overlap:
\begin{equation}
    S(h) = I(h) \times \big(1 - F(h)\big)^\alpha,
    \label{eq:score}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha \ge 0$ controls the strength of the recoverability penalty ($\alpha{=}0$ reduces to standard importance-only pruning).
Structures with the lowest $S(h)$ are pruned up to the target sparsity ratio.

The scoring captures three regimes:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{High $I$, low $F$} (irreplaceable scaffolding): No other structure can assume its function. High $S$ $\rightarrow$ \emph{protected}.
    \item \textbf{High $I$, high $F$} (redundant but important): RLVR can redistribute its function via global exploration. Low $S$ $\rightarrow$ \emph{pruned preferentially}.
    \item \textbf{Low $I$} (unimportant): Pruned regardless of overlap.
\end{itemize}

The key distinction from standard pruning---which ranks solely by $I(h)$---is that \rasp{} additionally down-weights protection of structures whose function overlaps with retained structures.
This is motivated by the finding that RLVR recovers through global exploration and path compression~\citep{rlsqueezes2026}, making high-overlap structures more amenable to functional redistribution than unique structures of equivalent importance.

\paragraph{Per-layer capacity floor.}
Global ranking by $S(h)$ can concentrate pruning in layers where many structures have high overlap, collapsing that layer's representational capacity.
To prevent this, we enforce a per-layer capacity floor: each layer must retain at least 40\% of its MLP channel groups after pruning.
For DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, this requires ${\ge}12$ of 28 MLP groups per layer.
During structure selection, if removing a candidate would violate this constraint, it is skipped in favor of the next lowest-scoring structure.
This constraint adds negligible overhead to the scoring procedure but prevents representational collapse in individual layers---a failure mode we observed when unconstrained global ranking was applied at 20\% sparsity.

Attention GQA groups and MLP channel groups are scored jointly and ranked in a unified list, enabling cross-component pruning decisions subject to the per-layer floor.
Algorithm~\ref{alg:rasp} summarizes the complete pipeline.

\subsection{Stage 3: Two-Stage Recovery}
\label{sec:stage3}

After pruning, we recover reasoning ability through two stages: supervised finetuning (SFT) warmup followed by GRPO-based RLVR.

\paragraph{Motivation for two-stage recovery.}
Directly applying GRPO to a pruned model fails.
Structured pruning disrupts the model's ability to generate end-of-sequence (EOS) tokens: in sampling mode, pruned models produce repetitive digit loops without termination, yielding sequences that are truncated at the maximum length.
Since GRPO computes rewards only from completed generations, this EOS collapse means no valid completions are produced, the clipped ratio reaches 1.0, and gradient norms drop to zero---making direct RLVR recovery impossible.

\paragraph{Stage 3a: SFT warmup.}
We first apply a short SFT phase on reasoning demonstrations to restore two capabilities destroyed by pruning: (1) EOS token generation, ensuring the model can terminate sequences, and (2) basic format compliance, so that outputs follow the expected answer extraction pattern.
SFT warmup uses teacher-forced training on chain-of-thought traces, which does not require the model to produce complete generations and thus bypasses the EOS collapse problem.
This stage is intentionally brief---its purpose is to restore the minimal generation competence needed for GRPO, not to recover full reasoning ability.

\paragraph{Stage 3b: GRPO recovery.}
With generation competence restored, we apply GRPO-based RLVR on mathematical reasoning data to recover the pruned model's reasoning ability.
The reward is binary: $r = 1$ if the model's final answer matches the ground truth, $r = 0$ otherwise.
RLVR is preferred over extended SFT for reasoning recovery because of their fundamentally different training dynamics: \citet{rlsqueezes2026} show that RLVR \emph{compresses} incorrect reasoning trajectories while SFT \emph{expands} correct ones.
For a pruned model, RLVR can globally explore and redistribute functionality across remaining structures, while SFT is limited to imitating fixed trajectories through a reduced architecture.
Structures with high functional overlap $F(h)$ are precisely those whose function can be absorbed by similar retained structures through RLVR's global optimization---the theoretical basis for \rasp{}'s scoring criterion.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning (\rasp{})}
\label{alg:rasp}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Model $\mathcal{M}$, calibration problems $\mathcal{D}_\text{cal}$, target sparsity $s$, overlap weight $\alpha$, SFT data $\mathcal{D}_\text{sft}$, RLVR data $\mathcal{D}_\text{rlvr}$, layer floor $\tau$
\Ensure Pruned and recovered model $\mathcal{M}'$
\Statex \textit{\% Stage 1: Reasoning-Trace Structural Analysis}
\State Load calibration pairs $\{(x_j, y_j)\}$ from $\mathcal{D}_\text{cal}$
\For{each structure $h \in \mathcal{H}$}
    \State Compute $I(h)$ via Eq.~\eqref{eq:importance}
    \State Collect activations $\mathbf{X}_h$
\EndFor
\For{each layer $\ell = 1, \ldots, L$}
    \For{each group $g_i \in \mathcal{G}_\ell$}
        \State $F(g_i) \gets \frac{1}{|\mathcal{G}_\ell|-1} \sum_{j \neq i} \text{CKA}(\mathbf{X}_{g_i}, \mathbf{X}_{g_j})$
    \EndFor
\EndFor
\Statex \textit{\% Stage 2: Recovery-Aware Scoring}
\For{each structure $h \in \mathcal{H}$}
    \State $S(h) \gets I(h) \times (1 - F(h))^\alpha$
\EndFor
\State Sort $\mathcal{H}$ by $S(h)$ ascending
\State $\mathcal{P} \gets \emptyset$
\For{each $h$ in sorted $\mathcal{H}$}
    \If{layer($h$) retains $< \tau$ fraction after adding $h$ to $\mathcal{P}$}
        \State \textbf{continue}
    \EndIf
    \State $\mathcal{P} \gets \mathcal{P} \cup \{h\}$
    \If{$\sum_{h' \in \mathcal{P}} \text{params}(h') \ge s \cdot |\theta|$}
        \State \textbf{break}
    \EndIf
\EndFor
\State $\mathcal{M}_\text{pruned} \gets \text{Remove}(\mathcal{M}, \mathcal{P})$
\Statex \textit{\% Stage 3: Two-Stage Recovery}
\State $\mathcal{M}_\text{warm} \gets \text{SFT}(\mathcal{M}_\text{pruned}, \mathcal{D}_\text{sft})$ \hfill $\triangleright$ restore EOS \& format
\State $\mathcal{M}' \gets \text{GRPO}(\mathcal{M}_\text{warm}, \mathcal{D}_\text{rlvr})$ \hfill $\triangleright$ restore reasoning
\State \Return $\mathcal{M}'$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}

\paragraph{Base model.}
We evaluate \rasp{} on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B~\citep{deepseek2025r1}, a 7B-parameter reasoning model built on the Qwen2.5 architecture~\citep{qwen2.5} with 28 transformer layers.
The model uses Grouped Query Attention (GQA;~\citealp{ainslie2023gqa}) with 28 query heads and 4 key-value heads per layer (group size $G{=}7$), and an MLP intermediate dimension of 18{,}944.
As a reasoning-distilled model whose chain-of-thought capabilities originate from reinforcement learning, it provides a direct testbed for studying how structural pruning interacts with RLVR-based recovery.

\paragraph{Evaluation.}
We evaluate on GSM8K~\citep{cobbe2021gsm8k}, a benchmark of 1{,}319 grade-school math problems requiring multi-step arithmetic reasoning.
We report exact-match accuracy: the final numerical answer is extracted from the model's generation and compared against the ground truth.
For rapid iteration during development, we use a fixed 100-problem subset ($N{=}100$); final results use the full test set ($N{=}1{,}319$).
We define recovery $\Delta$ as the accuracy gain from post-prune to post-recovery, capturing how much reasoning ability the recovery pipeline restores independent of initial pruning damage.

\paragraph{Hardware.}
All experiments run on a single node with 2$\times$ NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPUs (94\,GB VRAM each).
The two pruning variants (Standard and \rasp{}) are trained in parallel on separate GPUs to ensure identical wall-clock conditions.

\subsection{Pruning Configuration}
\label{sec:pruning_config}

\paragraph{Sparsity and granularity.}
We apply 20\% structured sparsity, removing entire attention GQA groups (each comprising 1 KV head and 7 query heads) and MLP channel groups jointly.
The MLP intermediate dimension (18{,}944) is partitioned into 28 equal-sized groups of 676 channels each.
Structures are scored and ranked in a unified list across both component types, enabling cross-component pruning decisions: a total of 395{,}232 parameters are removed under both Standard and \rasp{} configurations, ensuring matched parameter budgets.

\paragraph{Importance scoring.}
For each structure $h$, we compute Taylor-expansion importance $I(h)$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:importance}) using activations and gradients collected on 128 randomly sampled chain-of-thought examples from the GSM8K training set.
For GQA groups, we aggregate importance across the constituent query heads and KV head (Section~\ref{sec:stage1}).

\paragraph{CKA-based functional overlap.}
We compute intra-layer linear CKA (Eq.~\ref{eq:cka}) between all pairs of GQA groups and between all pairs of MLP channel groups within each layer, using the same 128 calibration examples.
The functional overlap $F(h)$ for each structure is the mean pairwise CKA with its within-layer peers (Eq.~\ref{eq:overlap}).

\paragraph{Recovery-aware scoring.}
The final pruning score $S(h) = I(h) \times (1 - F(h))^\alpha$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:score}) combines importance and functional overlap with $\alpha{=}1.0$.
Structures with the lowest $S(h)$ are pruned first, subject to the per-layer capacity floor.

\paragraph{Per-layer capacity floor.}
To prevent representational collapse in individual layers, we enforce that each layer retains at least 40\% of its MLP channel groups (${\ge}12$ of 28 groups per layer).
During structure selection, candidates whose removal would violate this constraint are skipped in favor of the next lowest-scoring structure.
This constraint is necessary because unconstrained global ranking at 20\% sparsity concentrates pruning in layers with uniformly high functional overlap, collapsing those layers' capacity.

\paragraph{Baseline: Standard importance-only pruning.}
As the primary baseline, we apply the same Taylor-expansion importance scoring and global ranking but without the CKA overlap penalty ($\alpha{=}0$ in Eq.~\ref{eq:score}), following LLM-Pruner~\citep{llmpruner2023}.
The same per-layer capacity floor and sparsity target are applied, ensuring that the only difference between Standard and \rasp{} is the head selection criterion.

\subsection{Recovery Pipeline}
\label{sec:recovery_pipeline}

After pruning, we apply a two-stage recovery pipeline (Section~\ref{sec:stage3}) consisting of SFT warmup followed by GRPO-based RLVR.

\paragraph{Stage 3a: SFT warmup.}
Structured pruning destroys the model's ability to generate EOS tokens, causing unterminated repetitive outputs that prevent GRPO from computing rewards.
We restore generation competence with a brief SFT phase on 75 chain-of-thought examples (approximately 1\% of the GSM8K training set), trained for 2 epochs (1{,}870 gradient steps) with teacher forcing.
This stage is intentionally minimal: its sole purpose is to restore EOS generation and basic format compliance, not to recover reasoning ability.

\paragraph{Stage 3b: GRPO recovery.}
With generation competence restored, we apply GRPO~\citep{shao2024grpo} on GSM8K training problems to recover reasoning ability.
The reward is binary: $r{=}1$ if the extracted final answer matches the ground truth, $r{=}0$ otherwise.
We use the following configuration: learning rate $1{\times}10^{-6}$, maximum completion length 1{,}024 tokens, 1{,}000 training samples, 1 epoch, KL penalty coefficient $\beta{=}0$, sampling temperature 0.7, and DAPO-style clipped loss.
Setting $\beta{=}0$ removes the KL divergence penalty against the reference policy, allowing the pruned model to freely explore new reasoning paths unconstrained by its pre-pruning behavior---a deliberate choice for post-pruning recovery where the reference policy's reasoning trajectories may no longer be achievable through the reduced architecture.

